-- title:
--   Section 2, Eq. (15) — under $\bar R$, $E(S_n/n) \to \max(\alpha,\beta)$
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ and $B$ be two populations whose expectations $\alpha$ and $\beta$ exist (are finite), let $1 = a_1 < a_2 < \cdots$ and $2 = b_1 < b_2 < \cdots$ be disjoint sequences of density $0$, and sample according to Robbins' rule $\bar R$. Then
--   $$\lim_{n\to\infty} E\!\left(\frac{S_n}{n}\right) = \max(\alpha, \beta),$$
--   where $E$ is the expectation under the sampling process generated by $\bar R$ and $S_n = x_1 + \cdots + x_n$.
--
--   This is the expectation form of display (14); Robbins notes that (14) "can be shown to imply" it. It turns the almost-sure statement into the statement about the expected average reward that the loss (9) is built from.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Finite expectations are the hypothesis that both reward laws are integrable, and nothing more. The expectation is the integral against the trajectory law `banditTrajMeasure ν (rule σ)`; under the integrability hypothesis $S_n/n$ is integrable for every $n$, so the integral is not a junk value.
-- source:
--   Robbins, Some aspects of the sequential design of experiments, Bull. Amer. Math. Soc. 58 (1952), p. 532, Section 2, Eq. (15)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RobbinsSeqDesign_ForcedSampling_Rule
import Definitions.Def_RobbinsSeqDesign_ForcedSampling_Loss

namespace RobbinsSeqDesign.ForcedSampling

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology BanditAlgorithm

/-- Robbins (1952), Section 2, Eq. (15), p. 532: for any two populations A, B whose
expectations `α`, `β` exist (are finite) and any pair of sequences (13), under the rule `R̄`,
`E(S_n / n) → max(α, β)`. -/
theorem expected_average_tendsto_max (ν : StochasticBandit 2)
    (hint : ∀ i, Integrable id (ν.P i)) (σ : ForcedSchedule) :
    Tendsto (fun n : ℕ => ∫ ω, partialSum n ω / (n : ℝ) ∂(banditTrajMeasure ν (rule σ)))
      atTop (𝓝 (max (banditArmMean ν 0) (banditArmMean ν 1))) := by sorry

end RobbinsSeqDesign.ForcedSampling
